---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import MedicalDisclaimer from '../components/MedicalDisclaimer.astro';

const title = 'eGFR vs Creatinine Clearance: Differences and When Each Is Used';
const description =
	'How eGFR (CKD-EPI, MDRD) differs from Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance: units, body surface area indexing, weight, drug dosing, and how to convert between them.';
---

<Layout
	title={title}
	description={description}
	modified="2026-10-23"
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'eGFR vs Creatinine Clearance', href: '/egfr-vs-creatinine-clearance' }]}
	schema={[
		{
			'@type': 'Article',
			headline: title,
			description,
			mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://gfrcalc.com/egfr-vs-creatinine-clearance#webpage' },
			dateModified: '2026-10-23',
			datePublished: '2026-10-23',
			author: { '@id': 'https://gfrcalc.com/#organization' },
			publisher: { '@id': 'https://gfrcalc.com/#organization' },
			image: 'https://gfrcalc.com/og-image.png',
		},
	]}
>
	<article class="container-page py-14 sm:py-20">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
			<Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'eGFR vs Creatinine Clearance' }]} />
			<h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
				eGFR vs creatinine clearance
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				Both numbers estimate how well the kidneys filter blood from a serum creatinine result,
				but they are calculated differently, reported in different units, and used for
				different jobs. Mixing them up is one of the most common errors when reading a kidney
				function result.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm text-faint">Last updated October 23, 2026</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Side-by-side comparison</h2>
			<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline">
				<table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
					<caption class="sr-only">Comparison of eGFR and creatinine clearance</caption>
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline bg-hairline-soft">
							<th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink"></th>
							<th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">eGFR (CKD-EPI, MDRD)</th>
							<th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Creatinine clearance (Cockcroft-Gault)</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline align-top">
						<tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Unit</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">mL/min/1.73m&sup2;</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">mL/min</td></tr>
						<tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Inputs</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Creatinine, age, sex</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Creatinine, age, sex, body weight</td></tr>
						<tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Body size</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Indexed to a standard 1.73m&sup2; body surface area</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Not indexed; scales with weight</td></tr>
						<tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Main use</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Detecting and staging CKD</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Drug dosing where labels specify CrCl</td></tr>
						<tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Staging</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Maps to KDIGO <a href="/ckd-stages" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">G1&ndash;G5</a></td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Not used for G-staging</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Why the body surface area matters</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				eGFR is expressed per 1.73m&sup2; of body surface area (BSA), the BSA of an average adult.
				That makes results comparable between people of different sizes, which suits CKD
				screening. A large person simply has more kidney tissue and a larger absolute filtration
				rate, so for dosing, the absolute figure is often more relevant. To convert an indexed
				eGFR to an absolute value for a given patient:
			</p>
			<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">eGFR (mL/min) = eGFR (mL/min/1.73m&sup2;) &times; patient BSA (m&sup2;) / 1.73</pre>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				For a person with a BSA of 2.0 m&sup2; and an indexed eGFR of 60, the absolute value is
				60 &times; 2.0 / 1.73 &asymp; 69 mL/min. Errors in this step were one cause of faulty results
				in the online calculators reviewed in the
				<a href="/methodology" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">2020 accuracy study</a> behind
				this site.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Which should be used for drug dosing?</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				Many drug labels and dosing guidelines were developed using Cockcroft-Gault creatinine
				clearance, so it remains widely cited for dose adjustment. Regulators and kidney
				organizations also accept eGFR-based dosing in many situations, particularly when the
				patient is close to average size. Which to use depends on the specific drug and on
				the patient's weight, so follow the product label and ask a pharmacist or prescriber.
				This site does not give dosing advice.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Limits of both</h2>
			<ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>Both rely on serum creatinine, which depends on muscle mass and diet.</li>
				<li>Cockcroft-Gault is less accurate in obesity, extremes of body weight, and rapidly changing kidney function.</li>
				<li>Neither is valid during acute kidney injury, when creatinine has not reached a steady state.</li>
				<li>Neither is designed for children. Pediatric estimates use the bedside Schwartz equation.</li>
			</ul>

			<p class="mt-8 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				You can compute both on the <a href="/" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">GFR calculator</a>;
				each result shows its unit and the working behind it.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Sources</h2>
			<ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>Cockcroft DW, Gault MH. Prediction of creatinine clearance from serum creatinine. <em>Nephron</em>. 1976;16:31&ndash;41.</li>
				<li>Inker LA, et al. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2021;385:1737&ndash;1749.</li>
				<li>Seiberth J, et al. Accuracy of freely available online GFR calculators using the CKD-EPI equation. <em>Eur J Clin Pharmacol</em>. 2020.</li>
			</ul>

			<div class="mt-12"><MedicalDisclaimer /></div>
			<RelatedLinks exclude="/egfr-vs-creatinine-clearance" />
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
